  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;grotesque&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">Critics called the sequel grotesque for running ten minutes long and leaning twice on the same joke.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The deep-sea tank&apos;s anglerfish, all needle teeth and a lantern sprouting from its skull, struck visitors as grotesque.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The ballerina&apos;s grotesque arabesque drew gasps from the highest balcony — a line so pure and effortless the audience forgot to breathe.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The grotesque farmhouse table, four centuries old and worn glass-smooth, anchored the inn&apos;s long dining room.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Grotesque means warped and misshapen to a disturbing degree, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because an overlong sequel with a recycled joke is a routine flaw — the word wildly overstates it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! Grotesque means distorted past the natural into something disturbing, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the anglerfish&apos;s needle teeth and skull-lantern look like anatomy rearranged by a bad dream.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Grotesque means misshapen and unnatural, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because a line so pure it stops the audience&apos;s breath is form perfected, not form warped.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Grotesque means twisted out of natural shape, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because the table is simply old and worn smooth — age and wear aren&apos;t distortion, and glass-smooth wood is the opposite of misshapen.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
